This is the final layer. You have built the validation engine and the core database processor. Now, we just need to route the HTTP traffic to them.

The golden rule of enterprise architecture is: **Controllers should be "dumb."** They should never contain business logic, parse files, or calculate data. A Controller's only job is to take an HTTP request, hand it to a Service, and return the HTTP response.

We are going to split your massive endpoint file into three clean, single-purpose controllers.

Create a new package called `com.fincore.VoucherService.Controllers` and add these three files:

### 1. `VoucherBulkUploadController.java` (File & State Management)

This handles the raw Excel file coming from the UI, checks the validation status, and downloads the error/success reports.

```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.Controllers;

import com.fincore.VoucherService.Dto.BulkUploadStateDto;
import com.fincore.VoucherService.Service.validation.VoucherBulkValidationService;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.web.multipart.MultipartFile;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.time.LocalDate;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/vouchers/bulk")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class VoucherBulkUploadController {

    private final VoucherBulkValidationService validationService;

    @PostMapping("/validate-init")
    public ResponseEntity<String> initializeUpload(
            @RequestParam("file") MultipartFile file,
            @RequestParam("postingDate") String postingDateStr) throws IOException {
        
        LocalDate postingDate = LocalDate.parse(postingDateStr);
        String requestId = validationService.initiateValidation(file, postingDate);
        
        // Return 202 Accepted because processing continues in the background
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.ACCEPTED).body(requestId);
    }

    @GetMapping("/status/{requestId}")
    public ResponseEntity<BulkUploadStateDto> getValidationStatus(@PathVariable String requestId) {
        BulkUploadStateDto state = validationService.getState(requestId);
        if (state == null) {
            return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
        }
        return ResponseEntity.ok(state);
    }

    @GetMapping("/download/{requestId}/{type}")
    public ResponseEntity<byte[]> downloadFile(@PathVariable String requestId, @PathVariable String type) {
        byte[] fileBytes = validationService.getFileBytes(requestId, type);
        
        if (fileBytes == null) {
            return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
        }

        String filename = "SUCCESS".equalsIgnoreCase(type) ? "Validated_Data.json" : "Error_Report.xlsx";
        MediaType mediaType = "SUCCESS".equalsIgnoreCase(type) ? MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON : 
                MediaType.parseMediaType("application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet");

        return ResponseEntity.ok()
                .header(HttpHeaders.CONTENT_DISPOSITION, "attachment; filename=" + filename)
                .contentType(mediaType)
                .body(fileBytes);
    }
}

```

### 2. `VoucherActionController.java` (The Write Operations)

This handles the final submission to the database and the Maker/Checker workflow approvals.

```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.Controllers;

import com.fincore.VoucherService.Service.core.VoucherCommandService;
import com.fincore.VoucherService.Service.core.VoucherWorkflowEngine;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/vouchers/actions")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class VoucherActionController {

    private final VoucherCommandService commandService;
    private final VoucherWorkflowEngine workflowEngine;

    @PostMapping("/process-batch/{requestId}")
    public ResponseEntity<String> processBatchToDatabase(
            @PathVariable String requestId,
            @RequestHeader("X-User-ID") String userId,
            @RequestParam String description) {
        
        // Starts the async chunk processor and returns the DB Batch ID immediately
        String batchId = commandService.createBatchFromCacheAsync(requestId, userId, description);
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.ACCEPTED).body(batchId);
    }

    @PostMapping("/workflow/{batchId}")
    public ResponseEntity<String> processWorkflow(
            @PathVariable String batchId,
            @RequestParam String action,
            @RequestHeader("X-User-ID") String userId,
            @RequestParam(required = false, defaultValue = "") String remarks) {
        
        workflowEngine.processWorkflowAction(batchId, action, userId, remarks);
        return ResponseEntity.ok("Workflow action '" + action + "' applied successfully to Batch: " + batchId);
    }
}

```

### 3. `VoucherQueryController.java` (The Read Operations)

This handles all the `GET` requests for dashboards, history, and summaries.

```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.Controllers;

import com.fincore.VoucherService.Models.RequestVoucher;
import com.fincore.VoucherService.Models.VoucherWorkflow;
import com.fincore.VoucherService.Service.core.VoucherQueryService;
import lombok.RequiredArgsConstructor;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

import java.util.List;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/vouchers/queries")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class VoucherQueryController {

    private final VoucherQueryService queryService;

    @GetMapping("/pending-checker")
    public ResponseEntity<List<RequestVoucher>> getPendingRequestsForChecker() {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(queryService.getPendingBatchesForChecker());
    }

    @GetMapping("/maker/{userId}")
    public ResponseEntity<List<RequestVoucher>> getBatchesByMaker(@PathVariable String userId) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(queryService.getBatchesByMaker(userId));
    }

    @GetMapping("/summary/{batchId}")
    public ResponseEntity<RequestVoucher> getBatchSummary(@PathVariable String batchId) {
        RequestVoucher summary = queryService.getBatchSummaryDetails(batchId);
        if (summary == null) {
            return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
        }
        return ResponseEntity.ok(summary);
    }

    @GetMapping("/workflow-history/{batchId}")
    public ResponseEntity<List<VoucherWorkflow>> getWorkflowHistory(@PathVariable String batchId) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(queryService.getWorkflowHistory(batchId));
    }
}

```

### The Refactoring is Complete

You have just transformed a monolithic, hard-to-debug "God Class" into a modern, distributed, domain-driven microservice structure.

* **Validation** is isolated and scales independently.
* **Database Writes** are wrapped in `REQUIRES_NEW` transactions to prevent locking.
* **Controllers** are lightweight traffic routers.

When you run this in your IDE, ensure all your `@Service` and `@RestController` annotations are correctly picked up by Spring Boot. Are you ready to compile and run your first test payload through this newly aligned architecture?
